---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import InsightCallout from '../../components/InsightCallout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/SectionHeading.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="ClearCase"
  description="ClearCase is a fully automated cartridge case triage solution for crime gun investigations. The product worked technically, but it had been built to prove a concept, not to support how investigators work in practice. I led a full UX/UI redesign to fix that, and in the process uncovered a critical gap in the workflow."
  tags={['B2B SaaS', 'UX Audit', 'Responsive Web']}
  heroImage="/images/projects/clearcase/thumbnail.png"
  accentColor="#2c3e50"
  previousProject={{ title: 'Unifying an Investigative Platform', slug: 'unified-platform' }}
  nextProject={{ title: 'GriefShare', slug: 'griefshare' }}
>
  <div class="case-study-body">

    <!-- Challenge -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="The Challenge"
          subtitle="ClearCase worked, but it had been built to a prototype standard: no design system, no responsive layout, and no real consideration for how investigators actually operate. I started with a comprehensive UX audit before touching anything, which surfaced deeper workflow gaps that would shape the entire redesign."
        />
        <p class="design-question"><strong>Design Question:</strong> How do you turn a technically capable but workflow-unaware tool into a product investigators actually trust?</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Before state -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="img-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio: 16/9;" data-label="Before - original app state · 16:9"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Redesign -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="The Redesign"
          subtitle="The original product had been ported from mobile to web without rethinking the layout, leaving investigators with unnecessary intermediary screens and too many clicks to reach key information. I redesigned every page around the increased screen real estate: tightening navigation, increasing information density for power users, and surfacing flexible exhibit views by firearm group or all at once."
        />
        <div class="img-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio: 16/9;" data-label="Redesigned navigation and layouts · 16:9"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Analysis Report -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="The Analysis Report"
          subtitle="The analysis report is the core deliverable of ClearCase, effectively the reason investigators use it. But the simplified version wasn't exportable, limiting how they could use it to build a case. I redesigned it to offer both versions on export, surfaced directly on the case detail page instead of buried behind multiple clicks."
        />
        <div class="img-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio: 16/9;" data-label="Analysis report - simplified and detailed versions · 16:9"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- A Gap Surfaces -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="A Gap Surfaces"
          subtitle="The UX audit flagged it, the redesign made it undeniable, and user feedback confirmed it: the web app had no way to add exhibits. Agencies were hesitant to rely on mobile for core casework, but mobile was the only entry point, leaving investigators at a dead end the moment they needed to act."
        />
        <div class="img-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio: 4/3;" data-label="Workflow mapping / gap analysis artifact · 4:3"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Web-Based Exhibit Creation -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="Web-Based Exhibit Creation"
          subtitle="Agencies were hesitant to rely on mobile because case data stored on a phone, whether personal or work-issued, can be subpoenaed as evidence, putting investigators at risk of losing their device during proceedings. In collaboration with engineering, we designed a flow that uses the phone's NFC reader to scan exhibits without storing anything locally, removing that risk. This workflow is now live in production."
        />
        <div class="img-placeholder" style="aspect-ratio: 16/9;" data-label="Exhibit creation flow · 16:9"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Reflection -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading title="Reflection" />
        <div class="reflections-grid">
          <div class="reflection-item">
            <h3>Audit before you redesign</h3>
            <p>Starting with a UX audit before touching the design meant the redesign was grounded in what was actually broken, not just what looked broken. The exhibit creation gap and the buried analysis report both came from that process, not from intuition alone.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="reflection-item">
            <h3>System as accelerator</h3>
            <p>Building on the LeadsOnline design system meant ClearCase felt native to the platform from day one. Shared tokens, components, and patterns cut design time significantly and gave the redesign a coherence it would have taken much longer to achieve from scratch.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="reflection-item">
            <h3>Know what the product is for</h3>
            <p>ClearCase has a lot of feature potential. The discipline was staying focused on what investigators actually needed: the analysis report and data-rich views that didn't waste their time. Designing for power users means cutting through what's possible to find what's essential.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .design-question {
    font-size: var(--text-body-lg);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    padding: var(--space-xl);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .design-question strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .reflections-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .reflections-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .reflection-item h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-h4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .reflection-item p {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  /* Placeholder image scaffolding */
  .img-placeholder {
    width: 100%;
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border: 2px dashed var(--color-text-muted);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .img-placeholder::after {
    content: attr(data-label);
    position: absolute;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-align: center;
    padding: var(--space-md);
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { initCaseStudyAnimations } from '../../scripts/scroll-animations';
  initCaseStudyAnimations('.case-section');
</script>
